---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { cameras, generated, num, countByCategory, CATEGORY_LABEL } from '../lib/alpr';
import fleetsData from '../data/fleets.json';
import retentionData from '../data/retention.json';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

// Read the finding straight out of the generated dataset rather than hardcoding
// it. The whole problem this page fixes is a headline number that drifted from
// the data behind it; computing it at build time makes that impossible.
const dataPath = new URL('../../public/data/cameras.json', import.meta.url).pathname;
const data = JSON.parse(readFileSync(dataPath, 'utf8'));

const alprTagged = cameras.filter((c: any) => c.source === 'alpr_tagged');
const tollInAlpr = alprTagged.filter((c: any) => c.category === 'toll');
const rtxMto = cameras.filter((c: any) =>
  c.operator === 'Ministry of Transportation of Ontario' &&
  /RTX|Raytheon/i.test(c.manufacturer || ''));
const pct = alprTagged.length ? Math.round((tollInAlpr.length / alprTagged.length) * 100) : 0;

const fleets = fleetsData.fleets;
const totalFleetVehicles = fleets.reduce((s: number, f: any) => s + (f.vehicles || 0), 0);
const peel = retentionData.entries.find((e: any) => e.operator === 'Peel Regional Police');
const toronto = retentionData.entries.find((e: any) => e.operator === 'Toronto Police Service');
const shortest = retentionData.entries.find((e: any) => e.j === 'New Hampshire');

const cats = countByCategory(cameras);
---
<Base
  title="Findings"
  titleFull="Four Findings About ALPR Surveillance in Canada"
  description="What the data shows once you separate tolling from policing: 40% of Canada's mapped ALPR surveillance is the 407, most scanning isn't on any map, and retention is a setting nobody outside each force checks."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Findings', href: '/findings' }]}>
  <h1>Four things the data shows</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every number here is computed from the published dataset at build time and links to the page
    where you can check it. If a figure below disagrees with the map, the map is right and this
    page is broken — tell us.
  </p>

  <section class="finding">
    <div class="fnum">01</div>
    <h2>Most of what Canada's ALPR map calls surveillance is the 407</h2>
    <p class="stat"><strong>{tollInAlpr.length}</strong> of <strong>{alprTagged.length}</strong> nodes tagged as ALPR surveillance in OpenStreetMap — {pct}% — are tolling infrastructure, not policing.</p>
    <p>
      Of those, <strong>{rtxMto.length}</strong> are the 407's own cameras: manufactured by RTX
      (Raytheon, which built the 407 ETR tolling system) and operated by the Ontario Ministry of
      Transportation. Every one of them falls inside the 407 corridor. They read plates because
      billing by plate is the service — you entered a toll road.
    </p>
    <p>
      Existing crowdsourced maps show these as generic surveillance cameras, which inflates the
      apparent scale of police surveillance by a wide margin. Readback classifies them separately
      and turns the layer off by default, because a toll you chose to pay is not a dragnet on
      your commute.
    </p>
    <p class="how">
      <strong>How to check it:</strong> filter <code>/data/cameras.json</code> for
      <code>source: "alpr_tagged"</code> and count <code>category: "toll"</code>. The
      classification rule is in <a href="https://github.com/mikeshoss/Readback/blob/main/scripts/build-cameras.mjs" rel="noopener">build-cameras.mjs</a>,
      and each node links to its OpenStreetMap record.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href="/">See it on the map, toll layer on →</a> ·
      <a href="/alpr/operator/ministry-of-transportation-of-ontario">The MTO cameras, listed →</a> ·
      <a href="/data">The data →</a>
    </p>
    <p class="dated">
      First identified 2026-09-01, when the same query returned 244 RTX/MTO nodes out of 618
      ALPR-tagged — 40%. The dataset has grown since; the finding held. Both figures come from
      the same rule applied to OpenStreetMap on different days.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="finding">
    <div class="fnum">02</div>
    <h2>The scanning that matters most is on no map at all</h2>
    <p class="stat">
      <strong>{fleets.length}</strong> Canadian police services run vehicle-mounted ALPR across
      roughly <strong>{num(totalFleetVehicles)}</strong> equipped vehicles.
    </p>
    <p>
      A dot map can only show cameras bolted to poles. But most Canadian ALPR capacity is mobile:
      cruisers scanning continuously while they drive. Those have no fixed location to map, so
      every existing ALPR map — ours included, until we added a capacity layer — structurally
      undercounts the thing it claims to measure.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why we render fleets as capacity circles rather than pretending not to know. It is
      also why a "how many cameras are there" framing is the wrong question.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href="/">See the fleet layer →</a> ·
      <a href="/tech">What the in-car systems collect →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="finding">
    <div class="fnum">03</div>
    <h2>Retention is a setting, not a standard</h2>
    {peel && toronto && (
      <p class="stat">
        <strong>{peel.r.split(' (')[0]}</strong> in Peel Region.
        <strong> {toronto.r.split(' (')[0]}</strong> in Toronto. Same province, same technology,
        neighbouring forces.
      </p>
    )}
    <p>
      Across the jurisdictions we track, how long a plate read of an innocent driver is kept runs
      from {shortest ? shortest.r : 'three minutes'} to indefinitely. Nothing about the hardware
      requires any of these numbers. Retention is an administrator's dropdown, and in most of
      Canada no statute constrains it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The consequence is the site's central claim: <em>every safeguard on these systems is a
      setting, and nobody outside each service checks what the settings are.</em> That is not an
      accusation that anyone is lying. It is an observation that nobody is in a position to know.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a href="/rules#retention">The full comparison, with sources →</a> ·
      <a href="/foi">The requests we filed to find out →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="finding">
    <div class="fnum">04</div>
    <h2>Some plate readers also listen for your phone</h2>
    <p class="stat">
      Two vendors sell ALPR units that additionally capture Bluetooth and Wi-Fi identifiers from
      passing vehicles. No Canadian retention rule mentions device signals at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      Jenoptik's TraffiCatch has been deployed in US border counties since around 2019. Leonardo
      markets comparable capability. More importantly, the radios needed to do this are already
      present in hardware from several major vendors — which means the capability is, again, a
      setting rather than a purchase.
    </p>
    <p>
      Retention rules, privacy impact assessments and oversight frameworks in Canada are written
      about plates. A device identifier is not a plate, and the rules are silent.
    </p>
    <p class="links"><a href="/tech#antenna">The antenna problem, sourced to certification filings →</a></p>
  </section>

  <h2>What is mapped, in total</h2>
  <div class="stat-row">
    {cats.map(([cat, n]) => (
      <div class="stat-box"><span class="n">{num(n)}</span><span class="l">{CATEGORY_LABEL[cat]}</span></div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>The limits of all four.</strong> Camera locations are crowdsourced from
    OpenStreetMap: somebody mapped them, which is not the same as anyone verifying them. The
    counts are a floor, not an inventory, and {num(cameras.filter((c: any) => !c.operator).length)} of
    {' '}{num(cameras.length)} records carry no operator tag at all. Newly <em>mapped</em> is not
    newly <em>installed</em>. Turning these into records a journalist can cite is what the
    freedom-of-information programme is for.
    {' '}Data © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL), generated {generated.slice(0, 10)}.
  </div>

  <p class="crumb">
    Press enquiries, data, or verification: <a href="mailto:themiltonrecord@gmail.com">themiltonrecord@gmail.com</a>
  </p>

  <style>
    .finding {
      background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.1rem 0; position: relative;
    }
    .fnum {
      font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      color: var(--toll); margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    }
    .finding h2 { font-size: 1.18rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
    .stat {
      font-size: 1.02rem; color: var(--text); border-left: 3px solid var(--toll);
      padding-left: 0.8rem; margin: 0.6rem 0 0.8rem;
    }
    .finding p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .how {
      background: var(--panel-2); border-radius: 8px; padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem;
      font-size: 0.85rem; margin-top: 0.8rem;
    }
    .how code { font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text); }
    .how a, .links a { color: var(--toll); text-decoration: none; }
    .how a:hover, .links a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    .links { margin-top: 0.7rem; font-size: 0.88rem; }
    .dated {
      margin-top: 0.7rem; padding-top: 0.6rem; border-top: 1px dashed var(--line);
      font-size: 0.82rem;
    }
    .stat-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.6rem; margin: 0.8rem 0 1.2rem; }
    .stat-box {
      background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem; min-width: 8.5rem;
    }
    .stat-box .n { display: block; font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text); }
    .stat-box .l { display: block; font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--muted); }
    .crumb { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
    .crumb a { color: var(--toll); text-decoration: none; }
  </style>
</Base>
